TIME ZONES


Unix has a "date" command which displays the date, day of the week, and time
This command also displays the time zone. As we supply the Unix,
the messages all display Eastern time. You may alter these messages.

         HOW TO CHANGE TIME ZONES

          The time zone is set in 2 places, and you should
	 adjust it in both places. The 2 files are:

		/etc/rc
		/etc/bcheckrc



	Simply find the TZ= line in these files, which looks like
		TZ=EST5EDT
		export TZ

	
       	There are 3 parts to the time zone, which has the format
	 XXXNYYY. The XXX represents the time zone to be
	 displayed for Standard Time, as in EST, CST, etc. The N
	 is the number of hours difference to Greenwich Mean
	 Time, and the YYY is the time zone to be displayed for
	 Daylight Time, as in EDT.

Just change the entry in both places, and everything should be
fine.
